onal Aut´onoma de M´exico,\nL6@P0: Apdo. Postal 20-364, 01000 Cd. M´exico, Mexico\nL7@P0: 2Department of Chemistry, University College London,\nL8@P0: 20 Gordon Street, London WC1H 0AJ, United Kingdom, EU\nL9@P0: 3Department of Physics, University of Gothenburg, 41296 Gothenburg, Sweden, EU\nL10@P0: (Dated: August 17, 2018)\nL11@P0: The accurate measurement of microscopic force fields is crucial in many branches of science and\nL12@P0: technology, from biophotonics and mechanobiology to microscopy and optomechanics [1–4]. These\nL13@P0: forces are often probed by analysing their influence on the motion of Brownian particles [4–7]. Here,\nL14@P0: we introduce a powerful algorithm for microscopic Force Reconstruction via Maximum-likelihood\u0002estimator (MLE) Analysis (FORMA) to retrieve the force field acting on a Brownian particle from\nL15@P0: the analysis of its displacements. FORMA yields accurate simultaneous estimations of both the\nL16@P0: conservative and non-conservative components of the force field with important advantages over\nL17@P0: established techniques, being parameter-free, requiring ten-fold less data and executing orders-of\u0002magnitude faster. We first demonstrate FORMA performance using optical tweezers [4]. We then\nL18@P0: show how, outperforming any other available technique, FORMA can identify and characterise stable\nL19@P0: and unstable equilibrium points in generic extended force fields. Thanks to its high performance,\nL20@P0: this new algorithm can accelerate the development of microscopic and nanoscopic force transducers\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1808.05468)\nciteturn21view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 16; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn19view0\",\"pattern\":\"maximum\"}); Total lines: 969\nL14@P0: we introduce a powerful algorithm for microscopic Force Reconstruction via Maximum-likelihood\u0002estimator (MLE) Analysis (FORMA) to retrieve the force field acting on a Brownian particle from\nL15@P0: the analysis of its displacements. FORMA yields accurate simultaneous estimations of both the\nL16@P0: conservative and non-conservative components of the force field with important advantages over\nL17@P0: established techniques, being parameter-free, requiring ten-fold less data and executing orders-of\u0002magnitude faster. We first demonstrate FORMA performance using optical tweezers [4]. We then\nL18@P0: show how, outperforming any other available technique, FORMA can identify and characterise stable\nL19@P0: and unstable equilibrium points in generic extended force fields. Thanks to its high performance,\nL20@P0: this new algorithm can accelerate the development of microscopic and nanoscopic force transducers\nL87@P2: = −k xn + σ wn, (2)\nL88@P2: where σn =\nL89@P2: q2Dγ2\nL90@P2: ∆tn\nL91@P2: and wn is a Gaussian random number with zero mean and variance ∆t\nL92@P2: −1\nL93@P2: n\nL94@P2: [12]. The central\nL95@P2: observation is that Eq. 2 is a linear regression model [9, 10], whose parameters, k and σ, can therefore be optimally\nL96@P2: estimated with a maximum likelihood estimator from a series of observations of the dependent (fn) and independent\nL97@P2: (xn) variables, as schematically illustrated in Fig. 1. The detailed derivation is provided in the Methods for the\nL585@P8: obtained by fitting F(x) to a linear function. The potential method requires a series of particle positions acquired\nL586@P8: over a time long enough that the system has equilibrated as well as the choice of the number of bins and of the fitting\nL587@P8: algorithm to be employed in the analysis: here, we used 100 bins equally spaced between the minimum and maximum\nL588@P8: value of the particle position, and a linear fitting.\nL589@P8: Power spectral density (PSD) analysis. The PSD method [4, 6] uses the particle trajectory in the harmonic\nL590@P8: trap, x(t), to calculate the PSD, P(f) = D\nL591@P8: 2π2 (f\nL592@P8: 2\nL593@P8: c + f\nL594@P8: 2\nL595@P8: )\nL596@P8: −1\nL597@P8: , where fc = (2πγ)\nL598@P8: −1k is the harmonic trap cutoff frequency,\nL599@P8: γ is the particle friction coefficient, and D is its diffusion coefficient. It then fits this function to find the value of k,\nL600@P8: and therefore the harmonic force field surrounding the particle. The PSD method requires a time series of correlated\nL605@P8: Auto-correlation function (ACF) analysis. The ACF method [4, 7] calculates the ACF of the particle position,\nL606@P8: C(τ) = kBT\nL607@P8: k\nL608@P8: exp \u0010−\nL609@P8: k|τ|\nL610@P8: γ\nL611@P8: \u0011\nL612@P8: , where kB is the Boltzmann constant and T is the absolute temperature. It then fits this\nL613@P8: function to find the value of k, and therefore the harmonic force field surrounding the particle. Like the PSD method,\nL614@P8: also the ACF method requires a time series of correlated particle positions at regular time intervals. It also requires\nL615@P8: to choose over which range to perform the fitting: here, we have performed the fitting over the values of the ACF\nL616@P8: larger than 1% its maximum.\nL617@P8: Experimental setups. For the single-beam (Figs. 2 and 3) and two-beam (Fig. 4) experiments, we used the\nL618@P8: standard optical tweezers shown in supplementary Fig. S1 [4, 28, 30]. An expanded 532-nm-wavelength laser beam\nL619@P8: (power at the sample 0.8 mW) is reflected by a spatial light modulator (SLM) in a 4f-configuration with a diaphragm\nL620@P8: in the Fourier space acting as spatial filter [4, 28]. By altering the phase profile of the beam we generate different\nL621@P8: beams, including the Gaussian beam used in Fig. 2, the Laguerre-Gaussian beams with l = −2, −1, 0, 1, 2 used in\nL622@P8: Fig. 3, and the double beam used in Fig. 4. We control the polarization of the beam using a quarter wave plate (QWP),\nL623@P8: which permits us to switch the polarisation state of the beam between linearly polarised (Figs. 3d and 3g), circularly\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Black-Box to White-Box: Control-Theoretic Neural Network Interpretability (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.12852v1)\nciteturn21view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn19view2\",\"pattern\":\"2.3\"}); Total lines: 583\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:     1. cite7†1.1 Objective L18:     2. cite8†1.2 Related Work on Neural Network Interpretability L19:     3. cite9†1.3 Contributions L20:   3. cite10†2 Methodology L21:     1. cite11†2.1 Problem Formulation L22:     2. cite12†2.2 Network Representation and State Definition L23:     3. cite13†2.3 Local Linearization L24:       1. cite14†2.3.1 Operating Point and Perturbations L25:       2. cite15†2.3.2 Layer Jacobians L26:       3. cite16†2.3.3 Global Input–Hidden Jacobian $B$ L27:       4. cite17†2.3.4 Hidden–Output Jacobian $C$ L28:     4. cite18†2.4 Gramian Construction L29:       1. cite19†2.4.1 Controllability Gramian L30:       2. cite20†2.4.2 Observability Gramian L31:     5. cite21†2.5 Hankel Singular Values and Mode Analysis L32:       1. cite22†2.5.1 Neuron-Level Importance L33:     6. cite23†2.6 Algorithmic Summary L34:   4. cite24†3 Result L35:     1. cite25†3.1 Example 1: $1$–$2$–$2$–$1$ Network with SwiGLU Activation L36:       1. cite26†Numeric illustration. L37:     2. cite27†3.2 Example 2: $2$–$3$–$3$–$2$ Network with GELU Activation L38:       1. cite28†Numeric illustration. L39:       2. cite29†Effect of operating point. L40:   5. cite30†4 Conclusion and Future Work L41:     1. cite31†4.1 Conclusion L42:     2. cite32†4.2 Future Work L43:   6. cite33†References L44: cite34†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L45: \nL46: arXiv:2511.12852v1 [cs.LG] 17 Nov 2025\nL47: # From Black-Box to White-Box: Control-Theoretic Neural Network Interpretability\nL48: \nL49: Jihoon Moon Affiliation: Department of Mechanical Engineering Affiliation: The Pennsylvania State University Affiliation: University Park, PA 16802 Email: jihoonmoon@psu.edu\nL51: Deep neural networks achieve state-of-the-art performance but remain difficult to interpret mechanistically. In this work, we propose a control-theoretic framework that treats a trained neural network as a nonlinear state-space system and uses local linearization, controllability and observability Gramians, and Hankel singular values to analyze its internal computation.\nL52: For a given input, we linearize the network around the corresponding hidden activation pattern and construct a state-space model whose state consists of hidden neuron activations. The input–state and state–output Jacobians define local controllability and observability Gramians, from which we compute Hankel singular values and associated modes.\nL53: These quantities provide a principled notion of neuron and pathway importance: controllability measures how easily each neuron can be excited by input perturbations, observability measures how strongly each neuron influences the output, and Hankel singular values rank internal modes that carry input–output energy. We illustrate the framework on simple feedforward networks, including a 1–2–2–1 SwiGLU network and a 2–3–3–2 GELU network.\nL129: where each $\\bm{\\sigma}_{\\ell}$ is activation function (e.g., SwiGLU, GELU, ReLU).\nL130: \nL131: We define the hidden state vector as the concatenation of all hidden activations:\nL132: \nL133:  | $$h=\\operatorname{col}\\big(h^{(1)},\\dots,h^{(L-1)}\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}},\\qquad n_{h}=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{L-1}n_{\\ell}.$$  |  | (6)\nL134: \nL135: Our objective is to derive a linear approximation\nL136: \nL137:  | $$\\delta h\\approx B\\,\\delta x,\\qquad\\delta y\\approx C\\delta h,$$  |  | (7)\nL138: around a fixed operating point $(x^{*},h^{*},y^{*})$, and then analyze this linear model using tools from linear systems theory.\nL139: ### 2.3 Local Linearization\nL140: #### 2.3.1 Operating Point and Perturbations\nL141: \nL142: Fix an input $x^{*}$ and compute the corresponding forward pass:\nL143: \nL144:  | $\\displaystyle z^{(\\ell)*}$  | $\\displaystyle=W_{\\ell}h^{(\\ell-1)*}+b_{\\ell},$  |  | (8)\nL145:  | $\\displaystyle h^{(\\ell)*}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\bm{\\sigma}_{\\ell}\\big(z^{(\\ell)*}\\big),\\qquad\\ell=1,\\dots,L-1,$  |  | (9)\nL146:  | $\\displaystyle z^{(L)*}$  | $\\displaystyle=W_{L}h^{(L-1)*}+b_{L},$  |  | (10)\nL147:  | $\\displaystyle y^{*}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\bm{\\sigma}_{L}\\big(z^{(L)*}\\big).$  |  | (11)\nL148: We define perturbations around this point as\nL149: \nL150:  | $$\\delta x=x-x^{*},\\qquad\\delta h^{(\\ell)}=h^{(\\ell)}-h^{(\\ell)*},\\qquad\\delta y=y-y^{*}.$$  |  | (12)\nL151: #### 2.3.2 Layer Jacobians\nL152: \nL153: For each hidden layer $\\ell$, define the diagonal activation derivative matrix\nL154: \nL155:  | $$D_{\\ell}:=\\operatorname{diag}\\big(\\bm{\\sigma}_{\\ell}^{\\prime}(z^{(\\ell)*})\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{\\ell}\\times n_{\\ell}}.$$  |  | (13)\nL156: \nL157: Using the chain rule,\nL158: \nL159:  | $$\\frac{\\partial h^{(\\ell)}}{\\partial z^{(\\ell)}}\\Big|_{x^{*}}=D_{\\ell},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial z^{(\\ell)}}{\\partial h^{(\\ell-1)}}=W_{\\ell},$$  |  | (14)\nL160: \nL161: so the Jacobian of layer $\\ell$ with respect to its input is\nL162:  | $$J_{\\ell}:=\\frac{\\partial h^{(\\ell)}}{\\partial h^{(\\ell-1)}}\\Big|_{x^{*}}=D_{\\ell}W_{\\ell}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{\\ell}\\times n_{\\ell-1}}.$$  |  | (15)\nL163: \nL164: Thus small perturbations propagate layer-by-layer as\nL165: \nL166:  | $$\\delta h^{(\\ell)}\\approx J_{\\ell}\\,\\delta h^{(\\ell-1)}.$$  |  | (16)\nL167: \nL168: For the output layer, define\nL169: \nL170:  | $$D_{L}:=\\operatorname{diag}\\big(\\bm{\\sigma}_{L}^{\\prime}(z^{(L)*})\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{y}\\times n_{y}},$$  |  | (17)\nL171: \nL172: then the Jacobian of the output with respect to the last hidden layer is\nL173:  | $$C_{\\text{local}}:=\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial h^{(L-1)}}\\Big|_{x^{*}}=D_{L}W_{L}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{y}\\times n_{L-1}}.$$  |  | (18)\nL174: \nL175: If $\\bm{\\sigma}_{L}$ is linear, then $D_{L}=I$ and $C_{\\text{local}}=W_{L}$.\nL176: #### 2.3.3 Global Input–Hidden Jacobian $B$\nL177: \nL178: The multivariate chain rule gives, for each hidden layer $\\ell$,\nL179: \nL180:  | $$\\frac{\\partial h^{(\\ell)}}{\\partial x}\\Big|_{x^{*}}=\\frac{\\partial h^{(\\ell)}}{\\partial h^{(\\ell-1)}}\\frac{\\partial h^{(\\ell-1)}}{\\partial h^{(\\ell-2)}}\\cdots\\frac{\\partial h^{(1)}}{\\partial x}=J_{\\ell}J_{\\ell-1}\\cdots J_{1}.$$  |  | (19)\nL181: \nL182: We define the block input–hidden Jacobian for layer $\\ell$ as\nL183:  | $$B^{(\\ell)}:=\\frac{\\partial h^{(\\ell)}}{\\partial x}\\Big|_{x^{*}}=J_{\\ell}J_{\\ell-1}\\cdots J_{1}=(D_{\\ell}W_{\\ell})(D_{\\ell-1}W_{\\ell-1})\\cdots(D_{1}W_{1}),$$  |  | (20)\nL184: \nL185: where the order of the matrix product is crucial and follows the chain rule.\nL186: \nL187: Stacking all hidden layers, we obtain the full input–state Jacobian\nL188: \nL189:  | $$B=\\begin{bmatrix}B^{(1)}\\\\\nL190: B^{(2)}\\\\\nL191: \\vdots\\\\\nL192: B^{(L-1)}\\end{bmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}\\times n_{x}},$$  |  | (21)\nL193: where each row block $B^{(\\ell)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{\\ell}\\times n_{x}}$ captures how the $\\ell$-th hidden layer responds to input perturbations near $x^{*}$.\nL194: \nL195: Elementwise,\nL196: \nL197:  | $$B_{ij}=\\frac{\\partial h_{i}}{\\partial x_{j}}\\Big|_{x^{*}},$$  |  | (22)\nL198: \nL199: where $h_{i}$ is the $i$-th component of the stacked hidden state.\nL200: #### 2.3.4 Hidden–Output Jacobian $C$\nL201: \nL202: The output $y$ depends only on the last hidden layer $h^{(L-1)}$. In terms of the stacked state $h$, the hidden–output Jacobian is\nL203: \nL204:  | $$C=\\begin{bmatrix}0&\\cdots&0&C_{\\text{local}}\\end{bmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{y}\\times n_{h}},$$  |  | (23)\nL205: \nL206: where the nonzero block $C_{\\text{local}}$ occupies the columns corresponding to $h^{(L-1)}$, and earlier hidden layers have zero direct influence.\nL207: Collecting these results, the local linear approximation of the nonlinear network around $x^{*}$ is\nL208: \nL209:  | $$\\delta h=B\\,\\delta x,\\qquad\\delta y=C\\,\\delta h.$$  |  | (24)\nL210: ### 2.4 Gramian Construction\nL211: \nL212: We now use the linearized model to define controllability and observability Gramians for the hidden state. Although the model is static (no explicit recurrence in depth), these Gramians provide meaningful measures of how easily each neuron can be influenced by the input and how strongly it influences the output.\nL213: #### 2.4.1 Controllability Gramian\nL214: \nL215: For a discrete-time linear system\nL216: \nL217:  | $$h_{k+1}=Ah_{k}+Bu_{k},$$  |  | (25)\nL218: \nL219: the (infinite-horizon) controllability Gramian $W_{C}$ is defined as the unique positive semidefinite solution of the discrete Lyapunov equation\nL220: \nL221:  | $$W_{C}=AW_{C}A^{\\top}+BB^{\\top},$$  |  | (26)\nL318: At layer $\\ell\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ we use a SwiGLU activation $\\bm{\\sigma}_{\\ell}(\\cdot)$, applied to the pre-activation $z^{(\\ell)}$. For our analysis we only require that $\\bm{\\sigma}_{\\ell}$ be differentiable; we denote its derivative by $\\bm{\\sigma}^{\\prime}_{\\ell}$ and obtain it via automatic differentiation.\nL319: \nL320: The forward map is\nL321:  | $\\displaystyle h^{(0)}$  | $\\displaystyle=x\\in\\mathbb{R},$  |  | (36)\nL322:  | $\\displaystyle z^{(1)}$  | $\\displaystyle=W_{1}h^{(0)}+b_{1},$  | $\\displaystyle h^{(1)}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\bm{\\sigma}_{1}\\big(z^{(1)}\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2},$  |  | (37)\nL323:  | $\\displaystyle z^{(2)}$  | $\\displaystyle=W_{2}h^{(1)}+b_{2},$  | $\\displaystyle h^{(2)}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\bm{\\sigma}_{2}\\big(z^{(2)}\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2},$  |  | (38)\nL324:  | $\\displaystyle z^{(3)}$  | $\\displaystyle=W_{3}h^{(2)}+b_{3},$  | $\\displaystyle y$  | $\\displaystyle=h^{(3)}=\\bm{\\sigma}_{3}\\big(z^{(3)}\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R},$  |  | (39)\nL325: where $W_{1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 1}$, $W_{2}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 2}$ and $W_{3}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times 2}$.\nL326: \nL327: We define the stacked hidden state\nL328: \nL329:  | $$h=\\operatorname{col}\\big(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}},\\qquad n_{h}=4.$$  |  | (40)\nL330: \nL331: For a given operating point $x^{*}$ we perform a forward pass to obtain $z^{(\\ell)*}$ and $h^{(\\ell)*}$ for $\\ell=1,2,3$, and compute the diagonal derivative matrices\nL332:  | $$D_{\\ell}=\\operatorname{diag}\\big(\\bm{\\sigma}^{\\prime}_{\\ell}(z^{(\\ell)*})\\big),\\qquad\\ell=1,2,3.$$  |  | (41)\nL333: \nL334: The layer Jacobians are\nL335: \nL336:  | $$J_{1}=D_{1}W_{1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 1},\\qquad J_{2}=D_{2}W_{2}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 2},\\qquad C_{\\text{local}}=D_{3}W_{3}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times 2}.$$  |  | (42)\nL337: \nL338: Using the general formulation of Section cite10†2 , the block input–hidden Jacobians are\nL339:  | $$B^{(1)}=J_{1}=D_{1}W_{1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 1},\\qquad B^{(2)}=J_{2}J_{1}=(D_{2}W_{2})(D_{1}W_{1})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 1},$$  |  | (43)\nL340: \nL341: and the full input–state Jacobian is\nL342: \nL343:  | $$B=\\begin{bmatrix}B^{(1)}\\\\[2.0pt]\nL344: B^{(2)}\\end{bmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{4\\times 1}.$$  |  | (44)\nL345: \nL346: In terms of the stacked state $h=\\operatorname{col}(h^{(1)}_{1},h^{(1)}_{2},h^{(2)}_{1},h^{(2)}_{2})$, the hidden–output Jacobian is\nL347:  | $$C=\\begin{bmatrix}0&0&C_{\\text{local}}\\end{bmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times 4},$$  |  | (45)\nL348: \nL349: so only the second hidden layer is directly observable from the output.\nL350: ##### Numeric illustration.\nL351: \nL352: To make these quantities concrete, we fix specific weights and biases for the $1$–$2$–$2$–$1$ network and evaluate the Gramians at a single operating point $x^{*}=0.5$. We choose\nL353:  | $\\displaystyle W_{1}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\begin{bmatrix}1.0\\\\\nL394: ### 3.2 Example 2: $2$–$3$–$3$–$2$ Network with GELU Activation\nL395: \nL396: Our second example considers a higher-dimensional setting with multiple inputs and outputs. Details of number of neurons and hidden layers are\nL397: \nL398:   * •\nL399: \nL400: $n_{0}=n_{x}=2$ (input layer),\nL401: \nL402:   * •\nL403: \nL404: $n_{1}=3$ (first hidden layer),\nL405: \nL406:   * •\nL407: \nL408: $n_{2}=3$ (second hidden layer),\nL409: \nL410:   * •\nL411: \nL412: $n_{3}=n_{L}=n_{y}=2$ (output layer).\nL413: We use the GELU nonlinearity at all hidden and output layers. The GELU activation $\\bm{\\sigma}_{\\ell}(\\cdot)$ with derivative $\\bm{\\sigma}^{\\prime}_{\\ell}(z)$ computed analytically or via automatic differentiation and used to form the diagonal matrices $D_{\\ell}$.\nL414: \nL415: The forward map is\nL416:  | $\\displaystyle h^{(0)}$  | $\\displaystyle=x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2},$  |  | (56)\nL417:  | $\\displaystyle z^{(1)}$  | $\\displaystyle=W_{1}h^{(0)}+b_{1},$  | $\\displaystyle h^{(1)}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\bm{\\sigma}_{1}\\big(z^{(1)}\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{3},$  |  | (57)\nL418:  | $\\displaystyle z^{(2)}$  | $\\displaystyle=W_{2}h^{(1)}+b_{2},$  | $\\displaystyle h^{(2)}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\bm{\\sigma}_{2}\\big(z^{(2)}\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{3},$  |  | (58)\nL419:  | $\\displaystyle z^{(3)}$  | $\\displaystyle=W_{3}h^{(2)}+b_{3},$  | $\\displaystyle y$  | $\\displaystyle=h^{(3)}=\\bm{\\sigma}_{3}\\big(z^{(3)}\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2},$  |  | (59)\nL420: where $W_{1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 2}$, $W_{2}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 3}$ and $W_{3}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 3}$.\nL421: \nL422: We define the stacked hidden state\nL423: \nL424:  | $$h=\\operatorname{col}\\big(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}},\\qquad n_{h}=6.$$  |  | (60)\nL425: \nL426: For a chosen operating point $x^{*}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2}$ we compute